WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. So in the middle of all the misery of human life it's easy to

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.460
forget that there is much worth celebrating and as we begin a new year I

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.820
thought it would be nice to start it off with some good news.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.740
Like for instance, divorce rate in the US have gone down every year since 2006 and

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.000
are currently at their lowest level since 1970.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Then there's pregnancy, which from what I can gather is not fun.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:27.000
The global rate of unintended pregnancy has declined dramatically since 1995 when 69 out of 1000 women

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.000
had unintended pregnancies. In 2008 it was 55 in 1000 women which is good news

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
because unintended pregnancies can have all kinds of health consequences

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
for infants and for mothers including increased infant mortality.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.500
Which reminds me that infant mortality is at an all-time low.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.580
Worldwide, it has dropped from 46 deaths per 1,000 births to 35 just in the last eight years.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.820
Now of course, you're worried that we're going to have like seventy gajillion humans on Earth,

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:56.000
but in fact, when infant mortality declines, families have fewer children.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.440
So decreasing infant mortality actually helps the overall population of humans stabilize,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.180
which is good because there are already too many of you driving up the price of the playoff tickets

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.660
from our hometown Indianapolis Colts going to the playoffs! More good news.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.100
But in more important good news, absolute poverty is down worldwide. In fact, the United Nations estimates

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:18.420
that poverty has decreased more in the last fifty years than it did in the previous five hundred.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.840
So much for the idea that colonization is good for the colonized.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.660
Adjusted for inflation, the number of people living on less than $1.25 a day has decreased from

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:31.540
43% in 1990 to 20% today. Now most of that is due to gains in China and India and Brazil.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.640
However, sub-Saharan Africa, long the world's poorest region, has been growing much faster than the world

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.520
economy for the past decade. Now inequality of wealth remains a huge problem around the world,

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.880
but in the vast majority of countries, the poor are becoming less poor.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.340
Also, there is good news for those of us in the developed world. In the United States, cancer mortality rates have

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:55.460
dropped by 20% in the last 25 years. Why? Well, mostly because people quit smoking, or died from smoking.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.940
But also, we have better treatments and screenings for colon and breast cancers and a bunch of others.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:03.240
Then there's the Internet, which is reaching more people at higher speeds. In the US, Internet speeds were up

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:09.020
27% in 2013 over 2012. In fact, they were up all around the world, from Guatemala, 75% increase,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.900
to Thailand, 32%. So it's easier than ever for people to stay in touch and to collaborate.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.460
It's also easier than ever to visit one another. While being herded like cattle into a steel cylinder

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.540
and rocketed through the air remains one of the least pleasant experiences that a human being can have,

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:28.940
airfares have dropped by almost 50% since 1985. You can also fly to more places than ever before,

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.820
and it's never been safer. Speaking of safe traveling, let's talk about cars.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.680
For every 100 million miles driven in the US in 2011, there was 1.1 vehicular fatality.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:43.960
In 1980, that number was 3.5. If you take 1980 as your baseline, safer cars and safer drivers saved

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:50.300
60,000 American lives in 2013. That's pretty great. Also things are getting better for nonhuman animals.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.260
Well, at least the ones that we've domesticated. Back in the 1990s, 17 million cats and dogs were euthanized

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.640
per year in the US. Now that number is closer to 5 million. What else? Well, the fight against malaria

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.140
is working, with mortality rates down nearly 50% in the last ten years.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:07.180
More girls are attending school around the world and the literacy rate for women in their twenties has risen

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:14.160
from 79% in 1990 to 87% today. And in almost every country on Earth, you are more likely to live to be old

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.560
than you were ten years ago. You are less likely to be hungry and you are likely better connected to those

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.200
you love. Hank, ten years ago, we couldn't have had this conversation because there was no YouTube.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.120
The Nerdfighter community couldn't have raised more than three quarters of a million dollars for charity

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.500
because there was no Nerdfighter community.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:34.300
Now of course, this progress is tenuous, and much of it is unsustainable with current technology,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:40.400
but it's also real and it's worth celebrating. So here's to a new year, Hank. May it really and truly be

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.560
the best one yet. Happy New Year!

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.840
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

